// A field's initializer was parsed, collected, and then dropped on the floor:
// an object's storage is zero-initialized and nothing ever ran what was
// written here, so `private int64 n = 5;` read back 0 and a string field read
// back null. Wrong answers, no diagnostic, on notation anybody would write.
//
// Refused rather than implemented, because what it would mean for a struct is
// a language question and not a backend one: a struct's storage is made
// without a constructor, and an element of `new S[n]` is zeroed with nothing
// to run. A static field's initializer is unaffected -- that one has always
// worked.

class Counter {
    private int64 seen = 5;
    private string label = "start";

    public int64 reading() { return seen; }
}

int32 main() {
    Counter* counter = new Counter();
    println(format("{}", counter.reading()));
    delete counter;
    return 0;
}
